{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 25,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.57,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Tale of Three Intersecting Lines"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.087,
            0.777,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "3. Construct a right-angled triangle ∆ABC with ∠B = 90°, AC = 5 cm. How many different triangles exist with these measurements?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.794,
            0.077,
            0.894,
            0.139
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_25_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.154,
            0.848,
            0.211
          ],
          "content": "[**Hint:** Note that the other measurements can take any values. Take AC as the base. What values can ∠A and ∠C take so that the other angle is 90°?]"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.215,
            0.848,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": "4. Through construction, explore if it is possible to construct an equilateral triangle that is (i) right-angled (ii) obtuse-angled. Also construct an isosceles triangle that is (i) right-angled (ii) obtuse-angled."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.405,
            0.325,
            0.592,
            0.349
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "SUMMARY"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.359,
            0.823,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": "Use of a compass simplifies the construction of triangles when the sidelengths are given."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.397,
            0.823,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "A set of three lengths where the length of each is smaller than the sum of the other two is said to satisfy the triangle inequality."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.436,
            0.823,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": "Sidelengths of a triangle satisfy triangle inequality, and, if a given set of lengths satisfy the triangle inequality, a triangle can be constructed with those sidelengths."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.494,
            0.823,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": "Triangles can be constructed when the following measurements are given:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.531,
            0.624,
            0.55
          ],
          "content": "(a) two of the sides and their included angle"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.551,
            0.554,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "(b) two angles and the included side"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.57,
            0.655,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": "• The sum of the angles of a triangle is always 180°."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.589,
            0.823,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": "An altitude of a triangle is a perpendicular line segment from a vertex to its opposite side."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.627,
            0.823,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": "Equilateral triangles have sides of equal length. Isosceles triangles have two sides of equal length. Scalene triangles have sides of three different lengths."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.685,
            0.824,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": "Triangles are classified based on their angle measures as acute-angled, right-angled and obtuse-angled triangles."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.808,
            0.938,
            0.844,
            0.955
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.971,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
